onal number?","options":["It is $a/0$ for an integer $a$.","It cannot be expressed using integers.","It is only a positive ratio of integers.","It is $a/b$ for integers $a,b$ with $b\\neq0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"30913f14090f8f32fe341d06","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rational_number_f3c18b94_003_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a$ and $b$ be rational numbers. Which statement is always true?","options":["If $a<b$, then $(a+b)/2$ is a rational number with $a<(a+b)/2<b$.","If neither $a$ nor $b$ is an integer, then $a+b$ is not an integer.","The quotient $a/b$ is a rational number.","If $a<b$, then only finitely many rational numbers lie between $a$ and $b$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fde0b448984ce041c86477f3","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rational_number_f3c18b94_004_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression is undefined because its denominator is zero?","options":["$1/2$","$3/0$","$0/5$","$5/1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4ae26f707a147f3d666902e9","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rational_number_f3c18b94_005_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For nonzero integers $b$ and $d$, when do the fractions $a/b$ and $c/d$ represent the same rational number?","options":["When $a+b=c+d$","When $a=c$ only","When $ad=bc$","When $b=d$ only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73c67f4ab132c426901ad5b8","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_rational_number_f3c18b94_012_curated20261027_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Is the integer $-4$ a rational number?","options":["Yes, since $-4=-4/1$ is a ratio of integers with $1\\neq0$.","No, since integers and rational numbers form disjoint sets.","No, since a rational number needs a denominator other than $1$.","Only when it is written in a form such as $-8/2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"50bb4aa65c910eca5e0cfda1","time_limit":30},{"id":"rational_number_new_013_curated20261027_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb{Q}$ with $b\\neq0$. Which statement about the quotient $a/b$ is guaranteed?","options":["It is rational.","It is an integer.","It is irrational.","It is positive."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0418c356360c91d40dfa7b55","time_limit":20},{"id":"rational_number_new_017_curated20261027_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For rational numbers $a/b$ and $c/d$ with $b,d\\neq0$, which formula gives their sum?","options":["$\\dfrac{a+c}{b+d}$","$\\dfrac{ac}{bd}$","$\\dfrac{ad+bc}{bd}$","$\\dfrac{a+c}{bd}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"47fe73bba43ca9df78bf17b3","time_limit":25},{"id":"rational_number_new_019_curated20261027_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can the same rational number have infinitely many representations $a/b$?","options":["Multiplying numerator and denominator by the same nonzero integer keeps the value.","Adding the same integer to numerator and denominator keeps the value.","Multiplying only the numerator by a nonzero integer keeps the value.","Each rational number takes infinitely many different values."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3b6728cb4bde72b79977fb96","time_limit":25},{"id":"rational_number_new_020_curated20261027_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which rational number lies strictly between $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ and $\\dfrac{3}{5}$?","options":["$\\dfrac{1}{5}$","$\\dfrac{1}{2}$","$\\dfrac{4}{5}$","$\\dfrac{3}{5}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d074e928f47afd67614d373","time_limit":20}]}}